WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:03.840
“What are those things?” a member&nbsp;
of the exploration team asks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:09.520
pointing to hundreds of clear orbs. He&nbsp;
begins moving towards the mysterious objects.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:15.200
Progress is slow; the bulky spacesuit is meant&nbsp;
to withstand the planet's hostile conditions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:17.920
but doesn’t allow for much maneuverability.

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:22.480
“They look like canisters containing some&nbsp;
sort of creature,” the SCP agent says.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:25.600
Dr. Sanders points his headlamp&nbsp;
in the direction of the voice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:30.160
“Mother of God,” he whispers into his&nbsp;
helmet. “Stop moving!” he yells. “Are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:34.720
those eggs? Nothing is supposed to be able&nbsp;
to survive on this planet. That’s why it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:38.640
chosen for off-world testing and containment&nbsp;
for the Foundation's deadliest entities.”

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:44.000
The team points their lights at the orbs.&nbsp;
The creatures inside don’t seem to be moving.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:49.680
They are suspended in some type of gel, and when&nbsp;
the lights hit them, the creatures look like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:55.200
metallic squids. Dr. Sanders holds up his hand&nbsp;
to signal the rest of the team to stop moving.

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:58.960
“I thought I saw some activity,”&nbsp;
he says. The team looks around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:03.840
but they don’t see anything. “I must be jumping&nbsp;
at shadows. Grab the equipment, and let’s get out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:08.560
of here. We need to contact SCP command and find&nbsp;
out what they want us to do with these things.”

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:13.040
The team packs up their gear and makes their&nbsp;
way out of the cave system beneath the alien&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:18.560
planet's surface. As they exit the mouth of&nbsp;
the cave, they are greeted by a nighttime sky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:24.960
filled with stars and nebula. The undesignated&nbsp;
planet is located in the deep recesses of space.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:29.920
The only light on this part of the planet&nbsp;
comes from the suits of Dr. Sander’s team.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:36.080
Without any light pollution like on Earth, it&nbsp;
seems as if every part of the universe is visible.

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:40.880
“If it wasn’t so creepy here, it’d be kinda&nbsp;
beautiful,” one of the team members says.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:45.680
All of the SCP agents have stopped&nbsp;
walking and stare up at the cosmos.

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:48.800
“Come on,” says Dr. Sanders.&nbsp;
“Let’s get back to ILYENA and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:51.920
find out what the O-5 Council wants&nbsp;
us to do with this new discovery.”

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:56.960
The next night the exploration team is back at&nbsp;
the caverns where they discovered the mysterious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:02.320
orbs the previous evening. Dr. Sanders leads&nbsp;
his team into the winding tunnel system.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:07.200
It leads directly under one of the largest&nbsp;
mountain ranges on the planet. They approach the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:12.960
chamber that contains the clear orbs and get to&nbsp;
work collecting samples to bring back to the lab.

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:18.160
“We have no idea what these things actually are,&nbsp;
so be careful. If you see movement, immediately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:22.960
back away. I want teams of two for each pod. One&nbsp;
person collects the sample; the other holds the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:26.960
incinerator at the ready just in case you need&nbsp;
to fry whatever is inside of these things.”

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:32.240
The team members confirm their orders and get&nbsp;
to work. Everything goes according to plan.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:37.200
The samples are gathered, and the SCP&nbsp;
team makes its way out of the caverns.

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:41.520
When they get to the surface, Dr. Sanders&nbsp;
radios to the command center at ILYENA.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:44.880
“We are out from under the mountain. The&nbsp;
canisters have been secured. Estimated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:48.320
time to the rover is four minutes.&nbsp;
Will update when en route to base.”

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:52.160
“Roger that extraction team. We will have&nbsp;
the loading bay ready for your arrival,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:54.560
responds the communications officer at ILYENA.

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:58.560
Sanders and his crew board the rover and put&nbsp;
the canisters in the storage compartment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:03.440
The drive back to ILYENA is uneventful. The&nbsp;
entire planet is basically made up of dust&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:08.960
and rocks. Until yesterday, everyone at ILYENA&nbsp;
thought they were the only living things out here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:13.760
other than the few dangerous SCPs they&nbsp;
brought along with them to experiment on.

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:18.560
The rover pulls into the research base and&nbsp;
parks outside the airlock. The team carries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:23.600
the canisters into the storge dome for further&nbsp;
analysis before heading to decontamination.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:28.480
Sanders walks out of the airlock and takes off&nbsp;
his helmet. “Breen! We’ve brought back the orbs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:31.840
and they are awaiting your analysis in the&nbsp;
storage bay. I don’t know what they are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:35.840
but the fact that they might be alive will&nbsp;
be of great interest to the Foundation.”

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:40.400
“I look forward to examining the specimens. We’re&nbsp;
kind of backed up right now, but we’ll get around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:45.280
to it. You know, saving the best for last and&nbsp;
all.” researcher Breen responds. “There’s a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:50.160
delicate matter that we’re attending to at the&nbsp;
moment. It should be resolved soon.” Breen looks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:54.320
down at the clipboard he’s holding and frowns.&nbsp;
“I’ve got to go, but I’ll keep you updated.”

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:59.200
After a few days, Breen calls Dr. Sanders into his&nbsp;
lab. “You’re going to want to see this. It’s those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:05.280
weird egg-type specimens you brought back from&nbsp;
the caverns. They’ve been designated SCP-1124.”

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:07.520
“Perfect, I’ve been dying to&nbsp;
know what those things are,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:10.320
Sanders says as he enters the lab.&nbsp;
“Do you mind if I record this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:13.680
just so I have something to send back&nbsp;
to my superiors at the Foundation?”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:18.800
Breen agrees, and the two SCP researchers&nbsp;
begin their analysis of the clear orbs.

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:24.720
“So, they’re not made of glass like we originally&nbsp;
thought,” Breen begins. “We actually aren’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:29.040
entirely sure what these things are made of.&nbsp;
They definitely have the shape of an egg,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:34.400
but the solid, clear outer layer makes me think&nbsp;
that they are more like carrying containers than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:39.120
anything else. See, look.” Bree grabs the&nbsp;
top of one of the orbs and pulls it off.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:44.240
There is a satisfying pop, like a Champagne&nbsp;
cork exploding out of the top of a bottle.

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:49.040
Dr. Sanders looks at the lid and then watches as&nbsp;
Breen puts the top back on the orb. “But that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:54.720
not the weirdest part,” Breen says. “Take a look&nbsp;
at what’s inside.” Sanders leans over the opened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.720 --> 00:05:00.000
orb and looks into it. The creature inside&nbsp;
looks like a silver squid. It does not move,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:05.360
but Sanders gets an uneasy feeling as if there is&nbsp;
something inherently dangerous and evil about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:11.200
metallic squids. “Come over here; I have some laid&nbsp;
out on the table for you to examine,” says Breen.

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:14.960
They walk over to another lab bench where&nbsp;
there are several creatures laid out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:20.240
Breen uses forceps to move the appendage of&nbsp;
one of the creatures. The limbs are segmented,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:26.480
but there is no sign of eyes or a mouth as far as&nbsp;
Sanders can tell. “What are they?” asks Sanders.

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:32.000
“No idea, but watch this.” Breen picks up one of&nbsp;
the creatures with the forceps by the tentacle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:36.160
Sanders expects the arm to be flexible&nbsp;
and rubbery like a squid's, but instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:41.280
they are stiff and rigid like a knife. When Breen&nbsp;
pics up the limb, the entire creature moves with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:47.120
it like a statue. He pulls out a scalpel and&nbsp;
looks at Dr. Sanders. “Watch this,” he says.

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:53.120
Nothing happens as Breen presses the blade&nbsp;
against SCP-1124’s exoskeleton. He pushes harder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:58.720
and harder, and harder. Eventually, the scalpel&nbsp;
breaks, and the blade clangs to the floor.

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:03.920
“We haven't been able to break the shell, so we&nbsp;
don’t know exactly what’s inside. But I have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:09.920
few other ideas on how to crack this puppy open,”&nbsp;
Breen says with a smile. He pulls out a blow torch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:15.920
and begins blasting the metallic squid with the&nbsp;
flame. There is no effect. Breen then brings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:21.200
out a vat of liquid nitrogen. He picks up one of&nbsp;
the creatures and places it inside the canister.

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:25.360
When he pulls it out, the instance&nbsp;
of SCP-1124 is covered in frost.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:29.280
Breen grabs a hammer off of one of&nbsp;
the lab tables and smashes it down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:33.680
onto the shell. There is a clank as if&nbsp;
the hammer struck a metal pole. Breen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:38.560
shakes his hand, which is now throbbing from the&nbsp;
reverberations sent through the hammer on impact.

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:43.040
“Maybe they aren’t alive at&nbsp;
all,” chuckles Dr. Sanders.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:46.000
“Maybe they’re just figurines&nbsp;
or statues or something.”

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:49.120
“That’s what I was starting&nbsp;
to think, too,” replies Breen.

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:56.240
“What a waste of time,” Sanders says. He hocks&nbsp;
a loogie and spits at SCP-1124 in disgust.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.240 --> 00:07:01.120
The spit hits the outer shell and slowly begins&nbsp;
to run down the side. Dr. Sanders and Breen take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:06.160
a seat at one of the tables. “It seemed like those&nbsp;
things were going to hold some real promise. Maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:08.350
even get us off of this rock,” Dr. Sanders says&nbsp;
as he lights a cigarette and offers one to Breen.

00:07:08.350 --> 00:07:11.200
“Yeah, the orbs and whatever is&nbsp;
inside of them are real duds,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:14.160
Breen commiserates. “Guess&nbsp;
it's time to focus on—.”

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:18.000
Suddenly one of the arms of the creature&nbsp;
that Dr. Sanders spat on begins to move.

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:22.080
“Did you see that?” Breen yells. “The&nbsp;
squid-thing, it just moved its arm!”

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:27.360
Breen and Sanders run to the lab table where&nbsp;
the instance of SCP-1124 slowly stretches its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:31.680
tentacles as if feeling its surroundings&nbsp;
to get a better idea of its location.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:34.400
Breen reaches out to touch&nbsp;
one of the metallic arms.

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:37.760
“Stop! Don’t touch it!” Dr. Sanders yells.

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:43.360
But it’s too late. The creature makes contact&nbsp;
with Breen’s skin. It instantly comes alive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:47.680
All of its tentacles are now moving&nbsp;
freely; the ends have sharpened tips.

00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:52.400
The creature jerks one of its tentacles, slashing&nbsp;
Breen’s neck. Blood splatters across the room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:59.280
covering several other instances of SCP-1124. As&nbsp;
the blood touches them, they slowly come to life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:03.120
Their tentacles move all around as if&nbsp;
searching for the source of the bodily fluid.

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:08.080
Dr. Sanders shrieks for help. He sprints towards&nbsp;
the door. But before he can reach it, one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:15.200
instances of SCP-1124 launches itself off a table&nbsp;
and impales its spiked arms into Sander’s back.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:20.800
He screams in pain. Tears fall down the sides of&nbsp;
his cheeks as his hand slams against the glass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:25.840
door that leads out of the lab. The creature&nbsp;
climbs up his back and over his head. It then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:31.040
pries Dr. Sander’s mouth open and squirms down&nbsp;
his throat. As he is torn apart from the inside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.040 --> 00:08:37.680
by the metal tentacles of SCP-1124, his hand&nbsp;
slides down the door leaving a bloody handprint.

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:41.120
The audio recorder continues to&nbsp;
run in the lab. It picks up the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:47.200
unsettling sounds of metal on metal and the&nbsp;
guttural screeches of the SCP-1124 creatures.

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:50.400
The glass door leading from the lab&nbsp;
to the rest of the ILYENA facility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:55.440
shatters. Instances of SCP-1124 come&nbsp;
streaming out. The researchers at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.440 --> 00:09:00.240
the base have no idea that one of the most&nbsp;
dangerous SCPs the Foundation has ever faced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:06.320
is now squirming along the halls of the facility,&nbsp;
looking for hosts to lay their eggs inside.

00:09:06.320 --> 00:09:11.360
Years go by without any communication from ILYENA.&nbsp;
The Foundation assumes something has gone wrong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:15.760
but that was the point of setting up the&nbsp;
base on a faraway planet in the first place.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.760 --> 00:09:19.680
If an experiment got out of&nbsp;
hand or a dangerous SCP escaped,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:23.520
the only people who would be in danger&nbsp;
were the agents and Class-D personnel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:29.520
at ILYENA. It was a much safer alternative to&nbsp;
conducting these sorts of experiments on Earth.

00:09:29.520 --> 00:09:34.080
The O-5 council redacts much of the information&nbsp;
on ILYENA and the research happening there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:39.200
The file is buried deep within the Foundation’s&nbsp;
database and forgotten. That is until several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:44.960
years later when a ship from ILYENA crash&nbsp;
lands just outside an Air Force Base in Nevada.

00:09:44.960 --> 00:09:48.640
“General! We’ve just received word from the&nbsp;
Foundation that one of our deep space research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.640 --> 00:09:52.400
vessels has crashed in the desert not too far&nbsp;
from here. The O-5 Council has asked that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:57.360
send a team to recover whatever is on board.&nbsp;
Apparently, it came from a base called ILYENA.”

00:09:57.360 --> 00:10:00.240
“Never heard of it,” responds&nbsp;
the General. “Alright, let’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:03.040
send a task force to go retrieve&nbsp;
whatever is on board that thing.”

00:10:03.040 --> 00:10:07.520
A group of SCP scientists and agents are&nbsp;
dispatched to carry out reconnaissance and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.520 --> 00:10:11.840
containment procedures for the crashed ILYENA&nbsp;
rocket. There is very little information on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.840 --> 00:10:16.400
what to expect as most of the reports and&nbsp;
documents about ILYENA are classified.

00:10:16.400 --> 00:10:19.440
As the team sets out to recover&nbsp;
whatever is on board the rocket,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:24.080
a reptile-like creature that has remained&nbsp;
dormant at the base for the last 30 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.080 --> 00:10:29.040
twitches. No one notices the slight&nbsp;
movement even though this SCP is heavily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:33.440
guarded and deemed one of the biggest&nbsp;
threats to humanity’s very existence.

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:37.360
“General, we’ve reached the crash site,” one&nbsp;
of the agents radios back to the airbase.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:42.080
“The rocket is in rough shape, but the cargo&nbsp;
hold seems to be intact. We are opening it now.”

00:10:42.080 --> 00:10:46.320
The extraction team uses welding torches to&nbsp;
cut through the outer lock of the capsule.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:48.960
They pull back the door and peer inside.

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:51.760
“What are those things?” one of the agents asks.

00:10:51.760 --> 00:10:54.160
As the flashlight moves across the cargo hold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.160 --> 00:10:59.440
the team can see hundreds of glassy orbs packed&nbsp;
tightly into the space within the rocket.

00:10:59.440 --> 00:11:03.600
“Well, let’s get them back to base so they&nbsp;
can be analyzed,” says the team leader.

00:11:03.600 --> 00:11:08.560
Using recommended containment procedures, the&nbsp;
extraction team carefully removes each of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.560 --> 00:11:14.080
orbs and loads them onto a transport helicopter.&nbsp;
All of the agents wear protective hazmat suits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.080 --> 00:11:17.840
and no one is allowed to directly&nbsp;
come into contact with the orbs.

00:11:17.840 --> 00:11:22.960
Once the helicopter is loaded, it takes off and&nbsp;
returns to the Air Force Base. The chopper lands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.960 --> 00:11:26.480
and a team of researchers load&nbsp;
the orbs into a containment pod&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.480 --> 00:11:29.680
which is then brought immediately&nbsp;
to the lab for analysis.

00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:33.600
“I finally found some information on what&nbsp;
ILYENA was and what these things might be,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:36.720
the lead scientists at the base&nbsp;
informs the other researchers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:40.080
“It took a lot of digging, but I’m&nbsp;
pretty sure the orbs are classified&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:45.280
as SCP-1124. As far as I can tell, they&nbsp;
contain some kind of squid-like statue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.280 --> 00:11:49.680
but the information from ILYENA is kind of&nbsp;
spotty. Not exactly sure what happened up there.”

00:11:49.680 --> 00:11:53.760
The other researchers listen as they’re briefed.&nbsp;
“We’re pretty much starting from scratch here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:57.520
so be careful and use proper containment&nbsp;
procedures as if you’re dealing with an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.520 --> 00:12:01.840
unknown SCP,” the lead scientist&nbsp;
finishes. The researchers nod to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.840 --> 00:12:05.520
indicate they’ve heard what their&nbsp;
boss has said and then get to work.

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:10.720
Deep underground, the reptilian creature still&nbsp;
lies dormant. There are slight fluctuations in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.720 --> 00:12:15.520
the thermal readings in its containment chamber,&nbsp;
but the beast does not seem to be awake. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.520 --> 00:12:21.040
SCP agents on duty note in their log that the&nbsp;
thermal sensors might need to be recalibrated.

00:12:21.040 --> 00:12:24.720
Two researchers enter a cleanroom&nbsp;
and lock themselves inside.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:28.880
They have brought in one of the&nbsp;
SCP-1124 orbs for further analysis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.880 --> 00:12:32.880
As the researcher who is holding the orb walks&nbsp;
towards the table in the middle of the room, he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.880 --> 00:12:38.000
accidentally trips. The other researcher reaches&nbsp;
out and tries to grab his colleague but misses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.000 --> 00:12:42.240
The orb falls to the ground; the researcher&nbsp;
scrapes his leg on the corner of the table.

00:12:42.240 --> 00:12:44.320
He doesn't notice the tear in his protective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.320 --> 00:12:47.840
suit where a small trickle of&nbsp;
blood drips onto the floor.

00:12:47.840 --> 00:12:51.280
“Ow, that really hurt,” the&nbsp;
researcher says, rubbing his shin.

00:12:51.280 --> 00:12:55.280
The orb roles in a circle on the&nbsp;
ground; it slowly comes to a stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.280 --> 00:12:58.960
right on top of a drop of blood&nbsp;
from the injured researcher.

00:12:58.960 --> 00:13:04.640
The orb begins to shudder. Both of the scientists&nbsp;
look on in shock as it rolls along the floor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.640 --> 00:13:08.480
banging into the containment chamber&nbsp;
walls as if looking for a way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.480 --> 00:13:12.080
out. The researchers jump to&nbsp;
their feet and run after the orb.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.080 --> 00:13:16.400
One of the scientists grabs the orb off the&nbsp;
ground and holds it tightly in both hands.

00:13:16.400 --> 00:13:20.720
Suddenly, a hole appears in the orb's&nbsp;
surface. The researchers look at one another,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.720 --> 00:13:25.840
their eyes wide in terror. The researcher holding&nbsp;
the orb puts his hand over the hole to cover it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.840 --> 00:13:29.920
He instantly recoils in pain as&nbsp;
something stabs him from within.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.920 --> 00:13:34.320
A metallic tentacle appears through the&nbsp;
hole in the orb, followed by a creature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.320 --> 00:13:39.200
with a squid-like body. The creature remains&nbsp;
motionless, sensing the room around him.

00:13:39.200 --> 00:13:42.400
The scientist not holding the orb races&nbsp;
towards the storage closet to find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.400 --> 00:13:46.240
something to cover the hole with. The&nbsp;
other researcher remains motionless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.240 --> 00:13:49.520
hoping that the creature will&nbsp;
just go back into its container.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.520 --> 00:13:54.320
Without warning, the creature launches itself at&nbsp;
the faceplate of the researcher holding the orb.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.320 --> 00:13:58.000
It smashes through the protective shielding&nbsp;
and enters the mouth of the researcher.

00:13:58.000 --> 00:14:02.480
The other scientist screams in terror. He runs&nbsp;
out of the containment chamber and slams down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.480 --> 00:14:07.120
the button to activate the failsafe to prevent a&nbsp;
containment breach. The room begins to fill with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.120 --> 00:14:13.600
mist and triflic acid. Alarms sound in the lab;&nbsp;
other SCP personnel come rushing in. They watch in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.600 --> 00:14:20.320
horror as the head of the researcher with SCP-1124&nbsp;
inside of him starts to expand. Blood begins to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.320 --> 00:14:27.280
pour out of his nose, eyes, and ears. Then with&nbsp;
a gruesome pop, the researcher’s head explodes.

00:14:27.280 --> 00:14:30.800
The researchers lean closer to the window&nbsp;
to get a better look at what’s happening.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.800 --> 00:14:36.320
They scan the room when suddenly the instance&nbsp;
of SCP-1124 slams into the observation glass.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.320 --> 00:14:41.520
The researchers jump back in surprise. A crack&nbsp;
runs the length of the observation window.

00:14:41.520 --> 00:14:44.480
At the same time the alarms are&nbsp;
going off in the research lab,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.480 --> 00:14:49.520
different alarms sound deep within the facility.&nbsp;
Sensors have picked up a huge jump in thermal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.520 --> 00:14:54.960
energy in the reptilian creature’s chamber,&nbsp;
along with the movement of its tail and limbs.

00:14:54.960 --> 00:14:59.840
Armed SCP Foundation agents run towards the&nbsp;
containment chamber. But when they are only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:59.840 --> 00:15:05.280
a few feet away, the blast door is forced off&nbsp;
its hinges and slams against the far wall of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.280 --> 00:15:13.200
the complex. The containment team screeches to&nbsp;
a halt as SCP-682 slowly lumbers out of its cell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.200 --> 00:15:17.840
and shakes its body. The creature locks&nbsp;
eyes with the agents, who are frozen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.840 --> 00:15:24.080
in shock. It curls back its lips to reveal&nbsp;
razor-sharp teeth; saliva falls to the floor.

00:15:24.080 --> 00:15:29.760
“Open fire!” one of the agents yells.&nbsp;
They fire a barrage of bullets at SCP-682,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.760 --> 00:15:33.840
but they seem to have no effect. The&nbsp;
creature stalks towards the agents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.840 --> 00:15:36.720
and uses its claws and teeth to tear them apart.

00:15:36.720 --> 00:15:41.600
SCP-682 runs through the facility,&nbsp;
killing every living thing in its path.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.600 --> 00:15:46.080
It bursts out into the sunlight, where it&nbsp;
sniffs the air. It then sprints toward the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.080 --> 00:15:50.400
high-density fence and bursts through it&nbsp;
as if it was nothing more than cobwebs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.400 --> 00:15:54.560
SCP-682 runs in the direction&nbsp;
of the crashed ILYENA rocket.

00:15:54.560 --> 00:16:00.720
A battalion of agents is deployed to stop SCP-682,&nbsp;
but the monster quickly destroys anyone and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.720 --> 00:16:07.600
anything in its path. It seems to be on a mission,&nbsp;
and nothing will stop it. When SCP-682 reaches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:07.600 --> 00:16:12.800
the rocket, it consumes the entire research&nbsp;
team that is there examining the spacecraft.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.800 --> 00:16:19.040
It then begins sniffing around the area and climbs&nbsp;
into the cargo hold. It is looking for something.

00:16:19.040 --> 00:16:25.280
Mobile Task Force Iota-5 approaches the rocket in&nbsp;
two teams. The first is on the ground in armored&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.280 --> 00:16:32.640
vehicles; the second is in a helicopter. They have&nbsp;
a plan. It seems that SCP-682 is looking for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:32.640 --> 00:16:39.040
orbs that were inside the rocket. For some reason,&nbsp;
they are incredibly important to SCP-682, and it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.040 --> 00:16:46.480
will stop at nothing to find them. SCP-682 is&nbsp;
acting like a mother searching for a lost child.

00:16:46.480 --> 00:16:52.240
Mobile Task Force Iota-5 has brought one of&nbsp;
the SCP-1124 orbs with them in hopes they can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.240 --> 00:16:57.920
lure SCP-682 back to its containment chamber.&nbsp;
When the first team approaches in the chopper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.920 --> 00:17:03.120
SCP-682 stops its search. The creature&nbsp;
sniffs the air and spots the helicopter.

00:17:03.120 --> 00:17:07.200
“Uh oh, I think he’s spotted us,” says the pilot.

00:17:07.200 --> 00:17:12.320
The ground force opens fire on SCP-682,&nbsp;
but the creature does not seem interested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.320 --> 00:17:18.800
in them. Instead, SCP-682 remains fixated&nbsp;
on the helicopter as if it knows there is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.800 --> 00:17:23.520
an orb on board. The creature leaps off&nbsp;
the rocket and begins running towards the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.520 --> 00:17:29.280
helicopter. The pilot turns the chopper&nbsp;
and flies away with SCP-682 in pursuit.

00:17:29.280 --> 00:17:35.040
They make it back to the air force base just ahead&nbsp;
of SCP-682. One of the agents jumps out of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.040 --> 00:17:41.600
helicopter with the SCP-1124 orb in his hands.&nbsp;
He races towards a mobile containment chamber,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.600 --> 00:17:45.520
where he places the orb at the&nbsp;
far end before turning to get out.

00:17:45.520 --> 00:17:49.440
When he looks up, SCP-682&nbsp;
is standing in the doorway.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:49.440 --> 00:17:57.040
Its chest heaves up and down. Its eyes are&nbsp;
wild. The SCP agent raises his gun and fires.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.040 --> 00:18:02.960
SCP-682 snarls and leaps into the containment&nbsp;
chamber. The whole thing shakes as the agent is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:02.960 --> 00:18:08.800
torn apart. His gunshots and screams are silenced&nbsp;
as the door to the containment chamber shuts.

00:18:08.800 --> 00:18:13.280
The fiasco known as the “Grey Dragon Incident”&nbsp;
is an embarrassment to the Foundation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:13.280 --> 00:18:20.480
The O-5 Council has everyone involved terminated.&nbsp;
SCP-682 is secured in a new containment facility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:20.480 --> 00:18:26.560
far away from SCP-1124. And the mysterious orbs&nbsp;
are placed in their own containment chamber&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:26.560 --> 00:18:29.920
and hidden away from the&nbsp;
world. The working hypothesis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:29.920 --> 00:18:37.600
is that SCP-1124 are the eggs of SCP-682,&nbsp;
but further tests will need to be done.

00:18:37.600 --> 00:18:42.800
And since both of these SCPs are extremely&nbsp;
dangerous, these experiments might need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.800 --> 00:18:48.880
be conducted on a faraway planet. Perhaps&nbsp;
it is time for the SCP Foundation to revisit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:48.880 --> 00:18:54.800
ILYENA once again. Let’s just hope, this&nbsp;
time, they don’t bring anything else back…

00:18:54.800 --> 00:19:00.560
Now watch “SCP-682 - Ways SCP Foundation Tried&nbsp;
to Kill Hard To Destroy Reptile” or check out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.560 --> 00:19:06.400
“How to Kill SCP-682 - Theories and Ideas”&nbsp;
for more on the monster we all know and fear!

